  "question": {
    "text": "In a single-slit diffraction experiment, the slit is illuminated by light of two wavelength $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$. It is observed that the 2 nd order diffraction minimum for $\\lambda_1$ coincides with the 3 rd diffraction minimum for $\\lambda_2$. Then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "hfjpv",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_2}=\\frac{2}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "UIqd2",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_2}=\\frac{5}{7}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "NaIAn",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_2}=\\frac{3}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "SEhqj",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_2}=\\frac{7}{5}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hint: $d \\sin \\theta=\\mathrm{n} \\lambda$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{n}_1 \\lambda_1=\\mathrm{n}_2 \\lambda_2 \\\\\n& \\frac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_2}=\\frac{\\mathrm{n}_2}{\\mathrm{n}_1}=3 / 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
